Emily: PR incoming for docs troubleshooting on 429s/timeouts — need to clarify concurrent inflight vs requests-per-minute.
Aj: where exactly? i skimmed earlier.
Emily: second para under 'Why 429s happen' uses 'too many open connections' which is ambiguous. propose we say 'concurrent inflight requests' (per-client concurrency) vs 'rate window' (requests/minute).
Samir: add a short ops note about idempotency keys for retrying write ops to avoid duplicates.
Kevin: i'll draft a small JS snippet showing exponential backoff + signal cancellation and a trim helper for context-window errors.
Emily: also need a python snippet. keep both aligned with the new SDK call pattern (chat.completions.create / client.generate style).
Aj: pasted a quick backoff snippet i used in support tickets — feel free to adapt:

```
// backoff + context trim (pseudo)
async function callWithBackoff(client, req, maxRetries=4, signal) {
  let tryN = 0;
  while (true) {
    try {
      return await client.chat.completions.create(req, { signal });
    } catch (err) {
      if (!isRetryable(err) || tryN >= maxRetries) throw err;
      if (err.code === 'context_length_exceeded') req.prompt = trimPrompt(req.prompt, 0.2);
      const backoff = Math.pow(2, tryN) * 200 + Math.random() * 100;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff));
      tryN++;
    }
  }
}
```

Emily: good. i'll tweak wording: "Prefer client-side concurrency limits (eg 4-8 inflight per client) for many small clients rather than relying on server burst. Use idempotency keys for non-idempotent ops. Trim context only when you can sacrifice prompt length." 
Samir: ops tip: when you see 429 spikes, check prefix caching and continuous-batching — a cache-miss storm looks like a sudden surge.
Kevin: i'll add a tiny table of common error codes: rate_exceeded, requests_per_minute, context_length_exceeded, concurrent_limit and brief one-line remedies.
